{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-17541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution* $z={y}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{z}{x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}{x}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}z & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-{x}^{2},\\,Q(x)=-{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}-{x}^{2}z) & ={e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\cdot (-{x}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx}-{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}{x}^{2}z & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}/3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}) & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3})\\,\\,dx & =-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\,dx \\\\ z{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3} & ={e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c9fdee66a2f4844fbf2f3e2dd6650964dc3fdd38163941cfd8e661094ecd21a"} {"question" "q-17541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}}}"] ["math" "y={(1+\\frac{C}{{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}})}^{2}"] ["math" "y={(2+Cx)}^{2}"] ["math" "y={e}^{{x}^{2}}+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{x},\\,y(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation*\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-3x\n$$\n\n*using the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-3x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-3x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx}+z & =-3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(xz) & =-3{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(xz)\\,dx & =-\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\ xz & =-{x}^{3}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition $y(1)=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{C-1}\\,⟹\\,C=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c9fdee66a2f4844fbf2f3e2dd6650964dc3fdd38163941cfd8e661094ecd21a"} {"question" "q-17558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{-2x}{1-3{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2x}{1+x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "y={x}^{2/3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-17558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{3}}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{-2}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y(1-x{y}^{2})\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{-3/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{-3/2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{z}^{-1/2}(1-x{z}^{-1}) \\\\ {z}^{-3/2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}{z}^{-1/2}(x{z}^{-1}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}z(x{z}^{-1}-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79781" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c9fdee66a2f4844fbf2f3e2dd6650964dc3fdd38163941cfd8e661094ecd21a"} {"question" "q-79781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "x"] ["math" "{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79781" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n(x+1)\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{y}^{1/2}(x+1)^{3}+2y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,x>-1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z=(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{1/2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The substitution $z={y}^{1/2}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z=(x+1)^{2},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(0)=\\sqrt{y(0)}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1},\\,Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int 1/(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)^{-1}} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>-1.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=(x+1)^{-1}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,z & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z) & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ z & =(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(0)=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(0) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (0+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{0}^{2}+0+C) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have \n\n$$\nz=(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2} & =(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c9fdee66a2f4844fbf2f3e2dd6650964dc3fdd38163941cfd8e661094ecd21a"} {"question" "q-79847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}"] ["math" "y=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x)"] ["math" "y=(x-1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}"] ["math" "y=(x-1)^{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}"] ["math" "y={(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-79847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-168148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\nx\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,,\\,x>0\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-168148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x})+{z}^{-1} & ={x}^{2}{({z}^{-1})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{z}{x} & =-x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}\\,z & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int 1/xdx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x{|}^{-1}} \\\\  & =|x{|}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUnder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot (-x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}z & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{z}{x}] & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{z}{x}]\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z}{x} & =-x+C \\\\ z & =-{x}^{2}+Cx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =-{x}^{2}+Cx \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{Cx-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-168148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0c9fdee66a2f4844fbf2f3e2dd6650964dc3fdd38163941cfd8e661094ecd21a"} {"question" "q-168148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063342/state.json" "file_sha256" "82473b0c5172d3de57abc36c653c173987e315860559381c03a4bb18edca2eed"} {"question" "q-168148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2x-C{x}^{2}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{Cx-{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{Cx}{{x}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{C{x}^{2}+1}{x}"] ["math" "